================ CONCLUSION ================
If STEP 3 (bare sum(;dims=k)) already NaNs while step-3-equivalent scalar `sum(A)` alone
would not, the culprit is Enzyme's reverse rule for `sum(A; dims=k)` on a Matrix -- a
documented historical weak spot, not anything specific to this project's economics.
If STEP 1 (withinTransform alone) NaNs but STEP 3 (bare dims-sums) does not, the culprit
is the COMBINATION (broadcast subtraction of a dims-reduced array back against the
original, i.e. `lz .- (sum(lz,dims=2)./D)`) -- an implicit broadcast/aliasing interaction,
not the reduction primitive alone.
